module gunbc.recurring_failure_mode.an_application_binds_by_position_where_the_model_binds_by_name

import std.types { NonEmptyStr }
import gunbc.recurring_failure_mode { RecurringFailureMode }

data an_application_binds_by_position_where_the_model_binds_by_name: RecurringFailureMode = RecurringFailureMode {
  identity: "an_application_binds_by_position_where_the_model_binds_by_name" as NonEmptyStr,

  receipts: [
    "TWO ROUTES ANSWER ONE QUESTION -- which argument feeds which parameter -- and they disagree about whether the children's SEQUENCE is part of the answer. The class is not a lowering bug: it is a fork of the binding AUTHORITY, and the model has already decided the question on the carrier that owns it, so the defect is that one route does not read what the model says.",
    "WHAT THE MODEL SAYS, structurally. A function's parameter set is the Arrow's DOMAIN, a `TypeNode { connective: Conj }` whose children are `Edge { label: Named { name } }` -- and `v2.std.node` `connective_edge_discipline` gives Conj `LabeledEdges`, which `canonicalize_labeled_for_content_hash` SORTS before hashing. So a parameter list's stored order is NOT identity-bearing: the parameter set is name-keyed, and the NAME is the binding key. The application is the other side: `behavior_edge_discipline` gives `Transform` `PositionalEdges`, which is not sorted, so the application carries an ORDER and, as lowered today, no names at all.",
    "WHERE THE FORK IS, cited by symbol. `v2.compiler.body_lowering_fold` `body_lower_named_arg_value_optional` decodes the `name ^dag_token_colon value` spine, matches the parameter binding as `_binding`, and returns ONLY the operand; `body_lower_call_arg_edge_optional` consumes that; `body_lower_transform_call` then builds the call as one operator edge plus one `Edge { label: Positional }` per argument. The name is READ and DROPPED at the one place it could have been carried. Downstream, `v2.compiler.infer` `infer_application_argument_inhabitance` recovers formals with `infer_formals_from_domain` (the Conj's STORED order) and arguments with `infer_transform_argument_targets` (`node_positional_child_targets`, stored order) and ZIPS them by index. So v2 binds declaration-order to call-site-order and consults no name. `v1.compiler.emit` `order_typed_call_args_from_semantics` does the opposite: it reads the `application_plan` that `v1.compiler.infer` built by parameter identity and renders arguments in FORMAL order, selecting each by `matched_argument_index`.",
    "THE SHARPEST STATEMENT OF THE DEFECT IS INTERNAL TO v2 AND DOES NOT NEED v1 AT ALL: the zip reads the Conj's stored order as if it were a fact, when the same module's content-hash canonicalizer sorts that order away. Two folds in one compiler disagree about whether a parameter list has a sequence. A node that round-tripped through canonicalization would rebind its own call sites differently.",
    "RUNG FOUND AT: below the floor. `applications bind in exact bijection` is a DESIGN section 4b floor sentence, and a binding chosen by a sequence the model does not carry is not a bijection the source wrote. The loss is SILENT wherever the mis-bound arguments share a representation -- which the v1 side already names in its own enrolled evidence: `v1.compiler_tests_rust` `ct_direct_call_formal_authority_carrier_test` asserts `matched_argument_index` explicitly because `positional carriage is silently wrong and still typechecks` when the two formals are both String.",
    "CEILING: structurally impossible, and the construction is nameable rather than aspirational -- an application whose arguments are keyed by parameter identity has no slot in which a sequence could be the answer. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER, NAMING THE CAPABILITY: the lowered application carries the argument NAME on the carrier that owns the binding, and every consumer (inhabitance, evaluation, emission) reads the binding from that one place -- so that dropping the name, or answering from child order, has no constructor. A v2 patch that carries the name AND keeps positional order as a second answer retires nothing: it makes the corpus carry both authorities instead of one.",
    "THE ENROLLED EVIDENCE ASSERTS THE DEFECT, NOT THE CONTRACT, AND SAYS SO IN ITS OWN TEXT. `test.claim.named_argument_binding_two_route_witness_test` `the_v2_route_binds_out_of_declaration_order_by_position_which_is_the_defect` is GREEN BECAUSE v2 IS WRONG -- it records that v2 refuses a call the source wrote correctly. It is a known-hole probe under DESIGN section 4b(4) and it FLIPS when the repair lands rather than retiring. It may not be cited as establishing that a reordered named call may refuse; the contract is the opposite, and a probe whose text does not say which of its two readings is the defect is how a hole calcifies into a cited behaviour.",
    "THE PART THAT IS NOT THIS LANE'S TO DECLARE, stated so the trigger is not mistaken for a small edit. Making a named call's identity independent of its argument sequence is a change to `v2.std.node` `behavior_edge_discipline` -- Transform would need the hybrid `canonicalize_arrow_labeled` already has (the operator edge positional and first, the named argument edges sorted) -- and that is the node identity law, which changes the content hash of every application in the corpus. What a mixed named/positional call does must REFUSE rather than widen (DESIGN section 5): there is no third authority that could say `these three by name and this one by the second slot`.",
  ],

  evidence: [],
}
